WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.234
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level B one.

2
00:00:05.534 --> 00:00:09.141
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.441 --> 00:00:14.810
Unit nine: Past Perfect (basic). Today you will show which past action happened first.

4
00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:17.484
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:21.608
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:23.149
Had.

7
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:27.020
Had means the helping verb of the past perfect.

8
00:00:27.617 --> 00:00:28.838
I had finished.

9
00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:31.673
Your turn. Say: Had.

10
00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:35.383
Had.

11
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.557
Past participle.

12
00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:41.502
Past participle means the third form of a verb.

13
00:00:42.117 --> 00:00:44.070
Eaten is the past participle.

14
00:00:44.683 --> 00:00:47.649
Your turn. Say: Past participle.

15
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:51.919
Past participle.

16
00:00:52.683 --> 00:00:53.545
Before.

17
00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:56.120
Before means earlier than.

18
00:00:56.717 --> 00:00:58.426
I had left before he came.

19
00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:01.727
Your turn. Say: Before.

20
00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:05.440
Before.

21
00:01:06.183 --> 00:01:07.463
Earlier action.

22
00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:11.355
Earlier action means the action that happened first.

23
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.961
The earlier action uses had.

24
00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.748
Your turn. Say: Earlier action.

25
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:21.891
Earlier action.

26
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.220
Two past actions.

27
00:01:25.217 --> 00:01:28.772
Two past actions means two events that both happened before now.

28
00:01:29.383 --> 00:01:31.511
Two past actions need an order.

29
00:01:32.117 --> 00:01:35.210
Your turn. Say: Two past actions.

30
00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:39.637
Two past actions.

31
00:01:40.383 --> 00:01:41.373
Already.

32
00:01:42.383 --> 00:01:45.056
Already means before a certain time.

33
00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.324
The film had already started.

34
00:01:47.917 --> 00:01:50.348
Your turn. Say: Already.

35
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.213
Already.

36
00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:55.707
First.

37
00:01:56.717 --> 00:01:58.890
First means before everything else.

38
00:01:59.483 --> 00:02:00.624
First, I had eaten.

39
00:02:01.217 --> 00:02:03.370
Your turn. Say: First.

40
00:02:06.233 --> 00:02:07.049
First.

41
00:02:07.783 --> 00:02:08.459
Then.

42
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:11.008
Then means after that.

43
00:02:11.617 --> 00:02:12.641
Then I went out.

44
00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.566
Your turn. Say: Then.

45
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:19.064
Then.

46
00:02:19.867 --> 00:02:23.448
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:24.283 --> 00:02:29.002
Visal, when you arrived, the film had already started. Which happened first?

48
00:02:30.117 --> 00:02:41.502
The film started first. We use the past perfect, had + past participle, for the earlier of two past actions. The later action uses the past simple.

49
00:02:42.617 --> 00:02:44.338
How do signal words help?

50
00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:56.160
Already, before, after, by the time and when show the order: By the time we arrived, the film had started. After he had eaten, he went out.

51
00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.331
Do we always need the past perfect?

52
00:03:00.417 --> 00:03:13.495
Not if the order is clear: After I ate, I left is possible. But the past perfect makes the sequence precise, and it is necessary when the earlier action explains the later one.

53
00:03:14.633 --> 00:03:16.424
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:17.283 --> 00:03:21.686
Grammar focus: Past perfect: had plus past participle.

55
00:03:21.986 --> 00:03:23.347
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.851
The film had already started when we arrived.

57
00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.459
After I had eaten, I went out.

58
00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:32.059
She had left before I called.

59
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:42.679
Use had + past participle for the earlier of two past actions. The later action uses the past simple. Signal words: already, before, after, by the time.

60
00:03:43.417 --> 00:03:47.288
Be careful! Don't say: When I arrived, she left already.

61
00:03:49.233 --> 00:03:51.953
Say: When I arrived, she had already left.

62
00:03:52.253 --> 00:03:54.879
Use the past perfect for the earlier action.

63
00:03:55.667 --> 00:03:58.458
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:59.267 --> 00:04:00.407
First and Then.

65
00:04:00.707 --> 00:04:03.276
When we arrived, the film had already started.

66
00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:06.104
After I had eaten, I went out.

67
00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:08.763
She had left before I called.

68
00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:12.950
By the time the bus came, we had waited for an hour.

69
00:04:13.967 --> 00:04:16.500
The earlier action uses the past perfect.

70
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.509
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.593
When I arrived, she blank left.

72
00:04:24.893 --> 00:04:27.908
A, has. B, had. C, was.

73
00:04:31.467 --> 00:04:33.153
The answer is B: had.

74
00:04:34.317 --> 00:04:36.421
After he blank lunch, he went out.

75
00:04:36.721 --> 00:04:40.432
A, had eaten. B, has eaten. C, eats.

76
00:04:43.967 --> 00:04:46.349
The answer is A: had eaten.

77
00:04:47.483 --> 00:04:51.427
Which action happened first?  'I had finished before she came.'

78
00:04:51.727 --> 00:04:55.589
A, I finished. B, she came. C, both together.

79
00:04:59.133 --> 00:05:00.994
The answer is A: I finished.

80
00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:03.743
Which sentence is correct?

81
00:05:04.043 --> 00:05:08.916
A, She had went home.. B, She had gone home.. C, She had go home..

82
00:05:12.467 --> 00:05:14.826
The answer is B: She had gone home..

83
00:05:15.983 --> 00:05:19.063
According to the reading, how long had they waited?

84
00:05:19.363 --> 00:05:23.086
A, an hour. B, a day. C, a minute.

85
00:05:26.633 --> 00:05:28.528
The answer is A: an hour.

86
00:05:29.733 --> 00:05:33.036
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:33.883 --> 00:05:38.112
Write a sentence using the past perfect about two things that happened in the past.

88
00:05:46.917 --> 00:05:48.510
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:59.812
When I arrived at the station, the train had already left. I had bought my ticket, but I was too late. I use the past perfect for the earlier action and the past simple for the later one.

90
00:06:00.112 --> 00:06:01.810
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:02.717 --> 00:06:08.422
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and past perfect: had plus past participle.

92
00:06:08.722 --> 00:06:12.698
Today's value is precision. Choose your words and numbers with care.

93
00:06:12.998 --> 00:06:15.360
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

94
00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:18.173
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

